<figure aria-labelledby="interpretation-caption">
  <ul class="interpretation-sequence">
    <li class="node">
      <strong>Define the question</strong>
      <small>Make the intended interpretation clear.</small>
    </li>
    <li class="node">
      <strong>Prepare the analysis population</strong>
      <small>Clarify which observations belong and what each represents.</small>
    </li>
    <li class="node">
      <strong>Distinguish descriptive and modeling samples</strong>
      <small>Keep the population behind each result visible.</small>
    </li>
    <li class="node">
      <strong>Estimate or compare associations</strong>
      <small>Describe relationships within the limits of the analysis.</small>
    </li>
    <li class="node">
      <strong>Compare groups and analytical approaches</strong>
      <small>Consider findings alongside other analytical views.</small>
    </li>
    <li class="node">
      <strong>Translate findings into qualified recommendations for review</strong>
      <small>Carry assumptions and limitations into the discussion.</small>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="interpretation-boundaries">
    <p>
      <strong>Interpretation boundaries</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Association does not establish causation.</li>
      <li>An analytical result is not a production prediction system.</li>
      <li>A recommendation does not prove adoption or impact.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <figcaption id="interpretation-caption" class="caption">
    This conceptual illustration demonstrates interpretation boundaries, not the
    historical studies or their results.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
